    "lawCode": "Add §260.35, Pen L",
    "lawSection": "Penal Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8229A\n\nSPONSOR: LANZA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the penal law, in relation to establishing the crime of\nmisrepresentation by, or on behalf of, a caregiver for children\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill establishes the crime of misrepresentation by, or on behalf\nof, a caregiver for children\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one provides that the act shall be known as \"Lulu and Leo's law\"\n\nSection two amends the penal law by adding a new section 260.35 in which\na person is guilty of misrepresentation by, or on behalf of, a caregiver\nfor a child or children when he or she:\n\n(a) intentionally makes a false written statement about himself,\nherself, or another person while he or she, or such other person, is\nbeing considered for employment, or while under employment as a caregiv-\ner, to a parent or guardian of a child or children, or the agent of a\nparent or guardian, and\n\n(b) such statement contains a materially false representation regarding\nthe caregiver's background related to the ability to safely care for a\nchild or children, and\n\n(c) a reasonable person would have relied upon such statement in making\nan employment decision\n\nSection three provides the effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn a tragic incident on the afternoon of October 25 2012, two children\nages six and two were stabbed to death by their nanny in an upper West\nSide Manhattan apartment. After returning home from a swimming lesson\nfor her three-year-old daughter, the mother found her two children bare-\nly breathing in a bathtub filled with blood.\n\nThe mother and father of the children hired the nanny based on false\nrecommendations that were written by the nanny's niece. The nanny was\nrecently convicted of four counts of murder but the people who gave her\nreference were not held accountable. This bill, named after the two\nslain children, would make offering a fraudulent child care employment\nreference a class A misdemeanor.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
